    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d28">

                <pb facs="0130" n="28" sameAs="frus:frus1933v01/pg28"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4/1755: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Acting Chairman of the American Delegation</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-03-10T17:00:00-05:00">March 10, 1933—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>298. Your 559, March 9, 1 p.m. and 561, March 9, 8 p.m. I am asking Grew, if he
                    is in a position discreetly to obtain any elucidation of the Japanese stand on
                    the Conference, to telegraph promptly. It might be useful for you to suggest to
                    Simon that he send similar instructions to Lindley.<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d28-f1"/> I agree with you that until the Japanese position is
                    thoroughly clarified we must exert especial caution.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d28-f1" n="55" xml:id="d28-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Francis Oswald Lindley, British Ambassador to Japan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
